title: Living documentation
section: Reports
desc: Turn your feature folders into one browsable page, coloured by the latest results, with bddr docs.
lede: Your feature files already describe what the product does. The docs command presents them as a readable page, so people who do not run tests can browse them.
---
<h2>Build it</h2>
<cb lang="bash">$ npx bddr docs -o reports/living-docs.html
$ npx bddr docs features --results reports/events.ndjson --title "Shop behaviour"</cb>
<div class="signature">
  <code>bddr docs [paths...] [options]</code>
  <p>Writes one offline HTML page.</p>
  <dl class="params">
    <dt>-o file</dt><dd>Where to write it. Default <code>reports/living-docs.html</code>.</dd>
    <dt>--results file</dt><dd>An NDJSON event file. Repeatable. Colours each scenario by its result. Without it, the latest recorded history run is used.</dd>
    <dt>--title text</dt><dd>The page title.</dd>
  </dl>
</div>

<h2>What you get</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A folder tree for navigation. Each folder's <code>README.md</code> is its introduction, and its first heading is the folder's title.</li>
  <li>Features with their descriptions, scenarios, steps, data tables and doc strings, and tags as chips.</li>
  <li>Search across all of it.</li>
  <li>With results, scenarios are coloured and each folder shows a roll-up.</li>
</ul>
<aside class="callout note"><p class="callout-title">Markdown</p><p>README files use a small, safe subset of Markdown. Links are limited to http, https, mailto and relative paths.</p></aside>
<figure class="shot"><img src="../assets/img/living-docs.png" alt="The living documentation page for the Playwright showcase: a folder tree with scenario counts on the left, and on the right the project title, a pass bar and the list of feature folders." width="1140" height="439" loading="lazy"></figure>

<h2>Publish it</h2>
<p>The page is one file. Publish it as a CI artifact, or copy it to any static host after each run on your main branch, so the documentation always matches the tests.</p>
